.\" CVEScanner's manual page: man ./cvescanner.1, or cvescanner.sh --man. Its options are those of
.\" cvescanner.sh --help (ManPageTest checks that it names every one of them).
.TH CVESCANNER 1 "2026-11-22" "CVEScanner" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cvescanner \- which known vulnerabilities apply to a project's dependencies, offline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cvescanner.sh
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIDIR\fR]
.br
.B cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIDIR\fR]
.br
.B cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui \-\-from\-analysis
\fIFILE\fR.cvescan [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B cvescanner.sh \-\-man
.SH DESCRIPTION
CVEScanner tells which known vulnerabilities apply to the dependencies of Maven, Gradle, npm and Python projects, to
the components off-the-shelf they ship (GDAL, FFmpeg, OpenSSL...) and to an installed product read from its binaries,
from vulnerability databases downloaded beforehand (the GitHub Advisory Database, the CVE List, OSV.dev's exports,
NVD's CVE feeds). It never reaches the network.
.PP
Without \fB\-\-no\-gui\fR it opens its window, scanning \fIDIR\fR at once when one is given. With \fB\-\-no\-gui\fR it
scans \fIDIR\fR (the current directory by default) and writes its reports: report.md, findings.csv, dependencies.csv,
findings.json and bom.cdx.json.
.PP
The launcher loads every database zip of the \fIdatabase\fR folder beside it and writes the reports in
\fIreports\fR beside it, unless the options say otherwise.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Scanning
.TP
\fIDIR\fR, \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
The directory to scan (default: the current directory).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-out\fR \fIDIR\fR
Where \fB\-\-no\-gui\fR writes its reports (default: ./cve-report; the launcher: reports/).
.TP
\fB\-\-db\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
Load every *.zip of \fIDIR\fR as a database (the launcher: database/).
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fIFILE\fR
Load a database from a zip file (repeatable): the GitHub Advisory Database, the CVE List (cvelistV5), an OSV.dev
ecosystem export or one of NVD's CVE feeds (JSON 2.0).
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\fR \fIFILE\fR
The ignore file of the findings reviewed (default: cvescanner-ignore.csv in \fIDIR\fR, when there is one); its
findings are left out of the counts and of \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\fR
Read no ignore file: report every finding.
.TP
\fB\-\-vex\fR \fIFILE\fR
A CycloneDX VEX document (repeatable; the *.vex.json of \fIDIR\fR are read anyway): its not_affected and
false_positive statements set their findings aside.
.TP
\fB\-\-db\-max\-age\fR \fIDAYS\fR
Warn when a database's newest record is older than \fIDAYS\fR (default 30; 0: never).
.TP
\fB\-\-maven\-repo\fR \fIDIR\fR
The local Maven repository, read for the transitive dependencies of the poms (default: the one ~/.m2/settings.xml
names, else ~/.m2/repository).
.TP
\fB\-\-gradle\-cache\fR \fIDIR\fR
Gradle's cache (its files-2.1 folder), read for the transitive dependencies of the builds without a lockfile
(default: under GRADLE_USER_HOME, else ~/.gradle).
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-only\fR
The declared dependencies only: neither the local Maven repository nor Gradle's cache read, the lockfiles' indirect
packages left out (alias: \fB\-\-no\-transitive\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-cache\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
Keep the databases' index between scans in \fIDIR\fR (the launcher: cache/ beside the program).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR
Read the databases from their zips at every scan.
.TP
\fB\-\-installed\fR
Scan an installed product from what it ships (jars, WARs, EARs, node_modules, site-packages, native libraries)
rather than source projects.
.TP
\fB\-\-cve\-only\fR
Only report the vulnerabilities that have a CVE ID.
.SS Scoring
.TP
\fB\-\-env\fR \fIPRESET\fR
The environment of the environmental CVSS score: none (default), internet, internal, airgapped, devci.
.TP
\fB\-\-reach\fR \fIR\fR
Override the reachability: internet, internal or local.
.TP
\fB\-\-cr\fR, \fB\-\-ir\fR, \fB\-\-ar\fR \fIV\fR
Override the confidentiality, integrity, availability requirement: L, M, H or X (not defined).
.TP
\fB\-\-cvss\fR \fI4\fR|\fI3\fR
The CVSS version preferred when an advisory has both (default 4).
.SS Output
.TP
\fB\-\-lang\fR \fIfr\fR|\fIen\fR
The language of the messages and of report.md (default: the program's setting, else French); the CSV and JSON files
are in English.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-gui\fR
Run without the window and write report.md, findings.csv, dependencies.csv (the dependency tree, a row each with its
path), findings.json and bom.cdx.json (a CycloneDX SBOM).
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\fR \fILEVEL\fR
With \fB\-\-no\-gui\fR: exit 2 when a finding is at or above LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH or CRITICAL.
.SS An analysis moved to another computer
.TP
\fB\-\-export\-analysis\fR \fIFILE\fR
Also write the scan as an analysis file (.cvescan): its dependencies, its findings, its ignore entries and VEX
statements, the databases it read. It holds the folder's paths and the dependencies' names.
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-analysis\fR \fIFILE\fR
Instead of a folder: an analysis file made elsewhere, its dependencies checked again against these databases. A
\fIDIR\fR ending in .cvescan does the same. \fB\-\-ignore\fR replaces its ignore entries, \fB\-\-no\-ignore\fR
drops them; \fB\-\-apply\-fixes\fR, \fB\-\-installed\fR, \fB\-\-maven\-repo\fR and \fB\-\-gradle\-cache\fR need
the folder itself.
.TP
\fB\-\-as\-scanned\fR
With \fB\-\-from\-analysis\fR: its findings as that scan found them, no database read.
.SS Filters (the reports, the counts and \-\-fail\-on keep only what they keep)
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-severity\fR \fILEVEL\fR
Only the findings at or above LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH or CRITICAL.
.TP
\fB\-\-ecosystem\fR \fILIST\fR
Only these ecosystems: Maven, npm, PyPI, COTS (comma-separated).
.TP
\fB\-\-hide\-approximate\fR
Leave out the findings on an approximate (≈) version.
.TP
\fB\-\-hide\-transitive\fR
Leave out the indirect dependencies (those another package brings).
.TP
\fB\-\-hide\-name\-matches\fR
Leave out the name matches (CVE records, jars without coordinates).
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR "\fIWORDS\fR"
Only the findings holding every word (package, CVE, file, summary...).
.SS Applying fixes (each project's own build tool, offline; nothing installed)
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\-fixes\fR
Plan the upgrades of the packages the report keeps and say them; nothing changes.
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Apply them, then scan again; exit 3 when one could not be applied.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-major\fR
Apply a major version change too (2.x to 3.x).
.TP
\fB\-\-fix\-packages\fR \fILIST\fR
Only these packages (comma-separated, as the report names them).
.SS The ignore file, edited without a scan (the one of DIR, or \-\-ignore FILE)
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-add\fR \fIID\fR
Add an entry setting \fIID\fR (a CVE or an advisory) aside; needs \fB\-\-reason\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-remove\fR \fIID\fR
Remove the entries naming \fIID\fR (with \fB\-\-package\fR or \fB\-\-version\fR: only those).
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-list\fR
List the entries.
.TP
\fB\-\-package\fR \fIP\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR \fIV\fR
The entry's package and version (default: any).
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR "\fITEXT\fR"
Why the finding is set aside.
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
The entry's last day (default: none).
.SS Help
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the options, in the language of \fB\-\-lang\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-man\fR
Show this manual (cvescanner.sh only).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The scan ran, no finding at or above \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR.
.TP
.B 1
An error: a wrong option, an unreadable database; \fB\-\-ignore\-remove\fR finding no entry.
.TP
.B 2
With \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR: a finding at or above its level.
.TP
.B 3
With \fB\-\-apply\-fixes \-\-yes\fR: a fix could not be applied.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I database/
The database zips the launcher loads.
.TP
.I reports/
Where the launcher writes the reports of \fB\-\-no\-gui\fR.
.TP
.I cache/
The databases' index, kept between scans.
.TP
.I ~/.config/cvescanner/cvescanner.properties
The window's settings (under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME when it is set).
.TP
.I cvescanner-ignore.csv
A scanned folder's own ignore file.
.TP
.I *.cvescan
An analysis file (\fB\-\-export\-analysis\fR, File > Export analysis), to open on another computer.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan a folder with a database, the reports written in another folder:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui \-d ~/src/my\-app \-\-db ~/cve\-db/Maven\-all.zip \-o ~/cve\-reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
~/cve\-reports then holds report.md, findings.csv, dependencies.csv, findings.json and bom.cdx.json. The launcher
also loads the zips of its \fIdatabase\fR folder; \fB\-\-db\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR loads those of \fIDIR\fR instead.
On Windows, cvescanner\-cli.bat takes the same options:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cvescanner\-cli.bat \-\-no\-gui \-d C:\esrc\emy\-app \-\-db C:\ecve\-db\eMaven\-all.zip \-o C:\ecve\-reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every database of a folder, several ecosystems at once:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui \-d ~/src/my\-app \-\-db\-dir ~/cve\-db \-o ~/cve\-reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan a project in a CI job, failing on a high finding (exit status 2):
.PP
.RS
.nf
cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui \-d . \-\-db\-dir /opt/cve\-db \-o cve\-report \-\-fail\-on HIGH
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan an installed product for an internet-facing environment:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui \-\-installed \-d /opt/shop \-\-env internet \-o ~/cve\-reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
On computer A, keep the scan in a file; on computer B, which cannot reach the folder, check its dependencies against
B's databases:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui \-d ~/src/my\-app \-\-db\-dir ~/cve\-db \-o ~/cve\-reports \-\-export\-analysis ~/my\-app.cvescan
cvescanner.sh \-\-no\-gui \-\-from\-analysis my\-app.cvescan \-\-db\-dir ~/cve\-db \-o ~/cve\-reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open the window on a folder, scanning it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cvescanner.sh ~/src/my\-app
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
The README beside the program, and https://github.com/j4ckk0/CVEScanner
